                "word": "péplos",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9441a01e-1ebe-3470-9796-07bfe3a86ddc",
                        "entry_id": "4789295d-f525-3429-9742-86b805761ba2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Tunique féminine de style dorien."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Ancient-Greek"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6a049255-f166-422f-a73e-67b4e2d1491a",
                                "sense_id": "9441a01e-1ebe-3470-9796-07bfe3a86ddc",
                                "text": "Chaque année, lors de la fête des Panathénées, on offrait à Athéna un péplos brodé."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "27ad414d-2264-447a-95d9-864064064cfd",
                                "sense_id": "9441a01e-1ebe-3470-9796-07bfe3a86ddc",
                                "text": "Hérodias apparut, — coiffée d’une mitre assyrienne qu’une mentonnière attachait à son front ; ses cheveux en spirales s’épandaient sur un péplos d’écarlate, fendu dans la longueur des manches."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d662a55e-411a-3c08-8514-d8cdc10a7066",
                        "entry_id": "4789295d-f525-3429-9742-86b805761ba2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Enveloppe d’un virus enveloppé."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f22e452a-cf09-40ca-8116-0e8dbcd546c7",
                                "sense_id": "d662a55e-411a-3c08-8514-d8cdc10a7066",
                                "text": "L’enveloppe (ou péplos) des virus enveloppés est une membrane constituée d’une double couche lipidique avec ou sans protéines."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "aac1d809-076a-4310-b7ed-3dba895f503f",
                                "sense_id": "d662a55e-411a-3c08-8514-d8cdc10a7066",
                                "text": "Tous les virus humains et animaux à capside tubulaire ont un péplos."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "064c47cc-b816-4323-bc73-42f3927fa256",
                        "source_entry_id": "4789295d-f525-3429-9742-86b805761ba2",
                        "source_sense_description": "Vêtement antique féminin",
                        "target_lang_word": "peplos",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "peplos",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "80567c93-f069-35e9-8756-a4b32e25e8d0",
                                        "entry_id": "ae6b5adb-9bff-31cd-801e-7240d754632f",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An Ancient Greek garment, worn by women, formed of a tubular piece of cloth, which is folded back upon itself halfway down, until the top of the tube is worn around the waist, and the bottom covers the legs down to the ankles; the open top is then worn over the shoulders, and draped, in folds, down to the waist."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5edc493d-449f-45b2-981b-5d387e25f2cd",
                                                "sense_id": "80567c93-f069-35e9-8756-a4b32e25e8d0",
                                                "text": "An obvious question arises: what on earth can Herakles do with a peplos? Throughout Greek tradition, the peplos (\"piece of cloth, veil, dress\") is a woman's garment, and sometimes a barbarian's garment—no contradiction, as far as a Greek is concerned."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6bef346b-76e5-4f3b-b5bb-eb47e657fa2e",
                                                "sense_id": "80567c93-f069-35e9-8756-a4b32e25e8d0",
                                                "text": "The garments (peploi) that serve the Trojan women as a pretext to laying hands on their victims are also the means of concealing the daggers that will kill […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "878ba095-0dc8-460e-b11a-ffac2c6eb62d",
                                                "sense_id": "80567c93-f069-35e9-8756-a4b32e25e8d0",
                                                "text": "I leave aside the vexed questions about whether one or two peploi were woven and presented to Athena during the Greater (every four years) and Lesser (every year) Panathenaic festivals […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
